To find the number of ways to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer from a math club of 20 members, where no member can hold more than one office, we need to find the number of permutations of 20 members taken 3 at a time.
This can be calculated using the permutation formula:
$$P(n, r) = \frac{n!}{(n-r)!}$$
In this case, $n = 20$ and $r = 3$.
$$P(20, 3) = \frac{20!}{(20-3)!} = \frac{20!}{17!} = 20 \times 19 \times 18$$
$$20 \times 19 \times 18 = 20 \times (19 \times 18) = 20 \times (19 \times (10 + 8)) = 20 \times (190 + 152) = 20 \times 342 = 6840$$
So, there are 6840 ways to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer from the 20 members.

