\textbf{Solution:}
Let $v = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}$ be a vector in $\mathbb{R}^2$. The projection of $v$ onto the $y$-axis is given by the formula
$$ \text{proj}_y(v) = \frac{v \cdot v}{\|v\|^2} v $$
We have $v \cdot v = x^2 + y^2$ and $\|v\|^2 = x^2 + y^2$. Therefore,
$$ \text{proj}_y(v) = \frac{x^2 + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} v = v = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} $$
The matrix that corresponds to projecting onto the $y$-axis is the matrix that multiplies the vector to obtain the projection.
Let $A$ be the matrix such that $A \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}$.
Then $A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}$.
